I mostly agree with you, it is perfectly possible and not at all=20
uncommon to ascend without reading the guidebook, but that was not=20
relevant to my point.

It was not my intention to say that 'reading the guidebook is a must to=20
ascend', rather that 'reading the guidebook is must because by reading=20
it between your games and consulting it as a reference while playing you=20
will find a lot of answers to your questions presented in a good and=20
newbie-friendly way, without spoiling and without requiring prior=20
knowledge about Nethack's inner workings'. My comment was directed=20
towards a 'newbie', trying to provide him with a good clue about how to=20
get deeper into the Nethack world. The discussion was not IMHO at the=20
'what should I read to really win this game' level of advancement.

A lot of people seem to think of reading the guidebook like an oh-
please-do-I-really-have-to-do-that chore, instead of a useful and fun=20
diversion between games to improve one's skill. I can't see the reason=20
for thie former attitude, even though I definitely don't think that it's=20
necessary to read the guidebook before you even start the game.

If people actually at least used the guidebook for reference we would=20
also have much less questions with obvious answers in RGRN, especially=20
the most uninteresting ones about configuration settings (which are=20
actually often from relatively experienced 'hackers who just can't be=20
bothered to check the guidebook).

The guidebook is also a good atmosphere-builder which gives you some=20
basics of Nethack-lore to get your imagination started when faced by the=20
ASCII-world.

I hope my intentions come through clearer with this posting. May I=20
recommend you reading the guidebook, because you're missing out if=20
you've never opened it? :)

(And the main reason I posted the text responded to was only to provide=20
the correct URL to the current version of the guidebook, something that=20
sort of got snipped out of the quoting. I then also made a comment=20
underlining the earlier poster's recommendation about reading the=20
guidebook.)

